  "worked_solution": "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $Y$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>0) & =P(Y\\in {-1,1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=-1)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =0.2+0.45 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
